{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.55,
      "why": "Pipelined gives 1 result/cycle and the shortest latency among my families (N+2). The system burst of 32 requests must drain within 0.44 us, so a high Fmax is essential; pipelined has the best chance of meeting sys_p99_batch_us. I search data_width 14..20 and n_iter 12..20 to cover the accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) while allowing area reduction. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small to limit area, and rounding is included because it can improve accuracy at no area cost."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 6, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "why": "Pipelined_m registers every m stages, reducing FF count at the cost of a longer combinational path and lower Fmax. It can also shorten latency to ceil(N/m)+2, which helps the burst deadline if Fmax remains high enough. I explore m=2..6 to find the area/latency/Fmax trade-off. The accuracy ranges match pipelined so the merged front compares like-for-like on error."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires 32 results within 0.44 us of a 1 MHz tick, i.e. a burst of 32 requests must drain in 0.44 us. With 1 result/cycle, this needs roughly Fmax >= (32 + latency)/0.44 MHz. Pipelined has the lowest latency and highest expected Fmax, so it gets the larger share. Pipelined_m may reduce LUT+FF area by sharing pipeline registers, but its lower Fmax could violate the system bound; exploring m=2..6 maps that risk. Accuracy needs at least 12 effective bits, so n_iter starts at 12 and data_width at 14 (output LSB 2^-12) to avoid wasting budget on clearly infeasible points. The ranges are deliberately moderate to let Optuna find the Pareto front within 100 evaluations, with later rounds reserved for refinement around the feasible region."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "Final round: the front is feasible and well-covered, so no further exploration is warranted. All three constraints are met by the front: throughput_msps best 273 (>=32, 0% violate), max_abs_err best 1.57e-05 (<=2.44e-04, 2^-15.96), and sys_p99_batch_us best 0.165 (<=0.44). The Pareto front spans luts_plus_ffs 1335..2768 against the HV reference 4000 and accuracy_bits 12.2..16 against the reference 12, i.e. it already covers the objective ranges with 9 feasible designs. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) is satisfied by the pipelined_m [data_width=17 n_iter=17 angle_guard=0 frac_guard=1 trunc m=4] design at luts_plus_ffs=1335, accuracy_bits=12.2, throughput 97.8 MSPS, comfortably inside all constraints. No constraint is unreachable, so infeasible is not appropriate; stopping preserves the mapped front."
  ,
  "next_plan": null
}
